#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365} :activity/id #uuid "3f795870-787b-5622-b078-947df7d3cd17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235 :topic/id #uuid "8b0ed26c-25c0-57a8-8d1c-6baa9735f5e3" :topic/math-academy-id 6373} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222492 :tutorial/content "We have seen how, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the current point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhile the implicit Euler method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the next point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nBoth have their advantages, but is there a way to combine them into one numerical method, applying the slope at both the current point and next point to update $y?$\n\nYes, there is! One such method is the trapezoidal method, which takes the *average slope in the interval between $x$ and $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$*\n\nMore formally, the **trapezoidal method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nLike the implicit Euler method, the trapezoidal method is an implicit numerical method. To apply the method, we substitute the formula for $\\Delta y$ into the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(f(x,y)+f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve the resulting implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$ directly, rather than explicitly calculating the value of $\\Delta y$ beforehand.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on IVPs whose slope function is linear in $y,$ i.e., of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can simply solve the implicit equation for $y_\\text{new},$ as demonstrated in the following worked example.\n\nFor more complex slope functions, we require a root-finding method, such as Newton's method, to solve the implicit equation iteratively. We'll cover this in a later lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1419612-b51a-5051-9478-0b5686cac1cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14490 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fc3f4329-6227-53b8-a848-08d6504d86a7" :step/math-academy-id 600181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222493 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nLet's use the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, in general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+1+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 = 1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 + 3\\cdot2 + 2= 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 9.$ We can view our approximation made by the trapezoidal method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a233830a75e76788ed9c4a5436ecb237aad6257591f0e20768c796195dca8eb2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c20c0100-eb93-50ee-b684-c56f4c9ec02d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14491 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "30729e81-2455-57d6-9939-77e4c4541d91" :step/math-academy-id 600182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221179 :question/id #uuid "62a1e90b-05a5-5828-abe0-fa5cbb4c1c1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308649}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "e6615bd4-7ab0-53c0-94f6-6ac2b5d6f381" :step/math-academy-id 600183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221178 :question/id #uuid "5399454b-0691-5cb3-884f-26af4ca52f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-21671"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372738}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "c9aae869-52a0-5af1-bbbd-93e447dc18db" :step/math-academy-id 600186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221180 :question/id #uuid "d387bb78-9df1-5551-9e1e-5a4745c5b731" :question/math-academy-id "e-21673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372753}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "250a7a8e-0521-5bf0-ad50-c0c51ae3eba8" :step/math-academy-id 600189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222532 :tutorial/content "Once the first iteration of a trapezoidal method implementation is complete, we can continue applying the same general update formula iteratively.\n\nIn our previous example, we used one iteration of the trapezoidal method on the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2,\n$$\n\nusing the general formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = x + x_\\text{new} + 3y + 2\n$$\n\nto approximate the value $y(1)\\approx9.$ Let's now approximate the value of $y(2)$ with one more step.\n\nSince the step size $\\Delta x = 1$ remains the same, the update formula derived in the previous step still applies.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have $x=1$ and $y=9.$ Hence, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 1+1=2,$ and the new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+2+3\\cdot 9+2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 32.$ We can visualize this second step as follows:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9752c16b15ebcfcfa214c775e99fb68139a1dfa9364a5f552fbf676d7953f7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "31f14503-26a9-5e09-b9cf-10a636f52db7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14898 :tutorial/title "Multiple Iterations of the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "b71ca947-77a6-59f8-9d4b-6ed77ec28832" :step/math-academy-id 608074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369}}] :activity/title "The Trapezoidal Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221465}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324} :activity/id #uuid "495fe43b-de66-5798-9669-4b55442f64d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881 :topic/id #uuid "c94f64e0-3901-584e-8de4-83ca8b66843b" :topic/math-academy-id 6378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222494 :tutorial/content "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/60/60be83e58a72728b03ebfa2de6999ec80f17ee29e3f2ab3060d8ca43bbf45de8.svg) and ![](images/98/98bb67f212fa8deee63da1b69ae93086b9eb56a3ec64d70ba36e6a94dff1527b.svg).\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n![](images/e6/e640b7ca9ddc8252223ec70f011147a702cd25bca0ee8a20314693695c1bec40.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/93/93058061ca42daa7114b615bcb77a8f9ccbc9cdccec98559db3eb288a7208940.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/72/72feeca197610c24c70b01f4c1075c75eebe89ae29f1aced83caeb252c275cce.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbbe8bd0-4cef-54d0-9803-a59eee727c6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6570665c-9c0e-5698-9c44-79ef60fe6401" :step/math-academy-id 591480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221182 :question/id #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444537}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"} :step/id #uuid "4ea90440-2d8e-5c27-b532-f6be5d45b3b4" :step/math-academy-id 591481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222496 :tutorial/content "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/7a/7a0ff677dae0e51e34aed63faadfcb31f277f060c03488fdc1828cf8a2a5a0ec.svg)\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9356e91f-f080-5827-a567-781aedac309a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14494 :tutorial/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "c10be260-d12b-595c-b241-523d377180f4" :step/math-academy-id 591484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221181 :question/id #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050780}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "264a8b35-ed46-5392-a8ec-e88ead83801a" :step/math-academy-id 591485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222495 :tutorial/content "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If \n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$ \n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8d4acde-0ca3-57a4-9994-a551a9d0094b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14493 :tutorial/title "Stability and Classification of Isolated Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "828feab6-cd8f-5d79-9356-a39ac111b298" :step/math-academy-id 591488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221183 :question/id #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" :question/math-academy-id "e-21676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510419}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"} :step/id #uuid "29c915bc-5c95-5594-b0cf-10edbe6f04a3" :step/math-academy-id 591489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222497 :tutorial/content "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case: \n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary. \n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d168b25-7771-5e55-8be8-2eb16080b9b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14496 :tutorial/title "A Note About Stable Centers"} :step/id #uuid "d039d8e2-81e7-5160-bcfd-7765a694f63a" :step/math-academy-id 591492}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221470}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510488} :activity/id #uuid "c7291f65-7a36-5e3f-9242-7648b94cd8a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048881 :topic/id #uuid "c94f64e0-3901-584e-8de4-83ca8b66843b" :topic/math-academy-id 6378} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a4fe5c5-df45-542c-8137-a773f298d44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368145} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca71f75-d635-5941-822d-a4497e7aa8a8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9275c133-5427-5250-a5e9-cd464196f7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368147} :answer-field/id #uuid "462a7a1d-db14-542b-ab8e-76df5f3ae7c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e00afc3b-0f8c-511a-ae3d-a6b6cdc86dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368149} :answer-field/id #uuid "832fa3f0-8f05-504a-b52e-696bd0e5e7a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4643b74-0553-58af-a597-f560a8bf34f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368151} :answer-field/id #uuid "24980e91-6228-5dad-a305-d83da3126fce" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "52a5f397-6ab6-5b30-a51f-fa3f7be9877d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335130" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ -3x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0) & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cb4bb92-6182-5d82-adf8-726f3c1f7eaa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ade299-a613-5a23-987e-23a091542225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509800} {:answer/id #uuid "866d6d61-ec02-5461-a9af-e13501b74bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509980} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d35f3f-1137-54b9-bd30-758c448db7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50e47a7-1678-588a-bc35-ad270c59097b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ef6b60-5a30-5b50-bc9b-fd1b1fc59d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510097} {:answer/id #uuid "f054de3f-c85f-5fce-886d-2896608021dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510204} :answer-field/id #uuid "c715942c-d2c2-5b4c-ae37-5167cb54c7d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "83d63d6d-d88e-5a5d-adf3-8972165ae385" :question/math-academy-id "q-336954" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=xy-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[2,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an  \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}-1)=x-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+5)=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x-2y \\\\ -2x & -2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}(1) & 2-2(1) \\\\ -2(2) & -2(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,1.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"} :step/id #uuid "372bc3a9-4bde-54ec-8f9c-e9a568ca29f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a55fdf-f65f-595c-83d0-6e0cb10b7741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510104} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb562b6-3ffd-5817-bd9b-9192877cbc82" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510271} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8397406b-3741-58c6-be48-cb822d70a432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509977} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1006ef8-7090-5491-a5a2-d9fd36d5275f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216ca193-a489-5830-9a58-4e49ef86790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509756} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b52cf4-5cc3-561a-bfeb-9f02941ae255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd4ad32-eeac-5002-877f-b890f2b10896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509912} :answer-field/id #uuid "f24f971e-0879-530e-bc3b-f3a08731a8aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59916f2b-317a-58b9-929b-11913732d3cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335133" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+{y}^{2})=4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-5y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-5y)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "70dd2bb3-126f-59ed-855a-f46120b28c5e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2047a8-8982-5263-b25f-46e01db0123d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368177} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3a8240-1762-543d-99d2-3372e89367f7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2da43c9-f82c-5c88-b1e6-691df9d8c9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368179} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3bca15-6c24-52f3-baa6-76c81a9a965b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57371dc2-00f9-5847-bcf0-bf21a9eee108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368181} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8f71a2-83e7-5924-ab0b-12a5484830f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2d6566-7c2c-5700-9daf-b1f1f46b1e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368183} :answer-field/id #uuid "3396f4c5-a888-5322-87d4-018f368e9efa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "396d0927-5f69-505a-80d1-a119800f214e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336900" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-6 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=x(t)-3y(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "819b91a6-5d0d-5137-8403-162591cc718d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ea5697-e4ef-54af-b81f-7e25b01bce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} {:answer/id #uuid "9810ecd9-fc7e-5f2f-a0da-32c49e519e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee66c07-aedb-5dc2-9782-17c5870e5ecd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc40fa7-8e1e-5f9b-ad4e-31114d9b1e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368206} {:answer/id #uuid "70f5be1c-7ce8-5c70-9672-5f4d7be10919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6e4ec8-2ac9-5ab9-907d-334bae85047c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368207} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a03ba21-b40c-5305-8798-d44f1ddced2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ce4d3c5-f9b2-510f-a42c-6aedb52f013f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337695" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t)+x(t)^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-2x(t)+y(t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ecc8f232-f4d0-52b1-8514-442214380c29"}] :activity/title "Linear Approximations Near Equilibria" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509690}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406} :activity/id #uuid "92d0371b-d4af-52c0-a1d3-83d95f7cf2d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048912 :topic/id #uuid "cbc6dbdd-cff6-5796-8970-556d22f811ac" :topic/math-academy-id 6397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222483 :tutorial/content "For a first-order initial value problem, we have already seen ways to approximate the increment in $y$ over a step, including:\n\n- the modified Euler method, which predicts using Euler's method and then corrects that prediction, and\n\n- the RK4 method, which combines several slope evaluations within a single step.\n\nAnother group of methods, called the **Adams-Bashforth-Moulton methods**, takes a different approach. Rather than computing multiple slopes at new points, these methods reuse slopes from previous steps in a predictor-corrector framework, giving high accuracy with relatively little additional work.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on one of the simplest: the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the **$2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method** (**ABM $2$-step**) with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ where $x_{-1} = x-\\Delta x.$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nSince ABM $2$-step uses the slope from the previous step, it cannot be started from a single initial condition alone. \nInstead, we initialize the method by computing the first step with a one-step method, typically the RK4 method.\n\nOnce the prediction $y_p$ is computed, we apply a corrector equation to refine our approximation. We'll return to this step shortly.\nBefore that, let's see an example of how to apply the predictor equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a2f37b-38d8-5a6f-bc13-9086af674ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14412 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5af445aa-9ec9-5489-9176-c4332bff0a03" :step/math-academy-id 611738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222510 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |  |  |\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c403981-071d-5b27-8ff8-99c1ea98715b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14615 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "fb2bf195-d713-5c11-b38c-0b93be4ea2b8" :step/math-academy-id 611739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221161 :question/id #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308373}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "183435e5-3131-5485-8b09-dfbc5b6bc6b9" :step/math-academy-id 611740 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222479 :tutorial/content "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe **$2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton** (**ABM $2$-step**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nThe final approximation for the new step is $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y.$\n\nSo, the corrector of the ABM $2$-step combines the slope evaluated at the predicted point with the slope evaluated at the current point and the previous point. Let's demonstrate how to apply the corrector equation to our previous example." :tutorial/id #uuid "684ff8c6-ca91-5e58-82e6-41d645b1e6db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14372 :tutorial/title "The Two-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method"} :step/id #uuid "78553a6e-4fa7-55a6-9f7b-b53ad1a3a237" :step/math-academy-id 611743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "098a5bb7-0ece-59ec-8400-a8e3efe1b00a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14385 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "702af9a1-f869-585a-9d62-8b4e15161407" :step/math-academy-id 611744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221164 :question/id #uuid "a185d6d7-e351-5f24-891a-aeb318cef584" :question/math-academy-id "e-21565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374683}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"} :step/id #uuid "f8c031d4-df22-535d-926c-b13ae32837bd" :step/math-academy-id 611745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221160 :question/id #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" :question/math-academy-id "e-21538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374711}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "a7c6fe9a-69d3-5418-8b88-993af0fa999e" :step/math-academy-id 611748 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221165 :question/id #uuid "ab9a026d-ddb2-52e8-9f52-4fbcf9b482a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398774}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "40865e2e-2c40-5544-acb9-196c8c726a9f" :step/math-academy-id 611751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222485 :tutorial/content "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2})|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from [math]0[/math] to [math]1.[/math]\n\nNotice that evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta = 0,[/math] we have\n\\begin{align*}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSimilarly, evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta=-1[/math], we get\n\\begin{align*}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]y'_{-1}[/math] is the slope at the previous point [math](x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).[/math]\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a *linear* interpolant using its values at [math]\\theta=0[/math] and [math]\\theta=-1.[/math]\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n\\begin{align*}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "dee56e00-8f80-598d-a5ea-b99a6322bddd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14478 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AB2 Predictor"} :step/id #uuid "d6baca6f-a078-54e6-b8c4-a3e52eb82476" :step/math-academy-id 611754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222486 :tutorial/content "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwe had $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x)).\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$ \n\n- For $\\theta = 0,$ we have $g(0) = y'.$\n\n- For $\\theta = -1,$ we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}.$\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$ But, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to $\\theta = 1{:}$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p).$\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p).\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta +1){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\theta -1)(\\theta +1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta -1){y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}q(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'})\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\frac{1}{3}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{-1}^{'}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{y}_{p}^{'}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n\\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n= \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aa97723-f737-5f47-be29-c04919e09c0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14479 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AM2 Corrector"} :step/id #uuid "5990a7fa-57e1-5d5e-9ae3-587f865f923b" :step/math-academy-id 611755}] :activity/title "The ABM2 Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221477}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231839} :activity/id #uuid "b8c77116-13b1-5351-9553-d047fa93cd04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049590 :topic/id #uuid "02cc8363-d667-570b-9679-eb87a01109a9" :topic/math-academy-id 6728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231995 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we solved second-order initial value problems using Laplace transforms when the forcing term was an ordinary function of $t.$\n\nIn this lesson, we extend the same method to **time-delayed forcing**, where the right-hand side turns on at a specific time. This type of forcing is modeled using the **unit step function**.\n\nFor a fixed constant $a>0,$ we write $$u_a(t) = u(t-a),$$ which represents a signal that is zero for $t< a$ and turns on at $t = a.$ For example, $u(t-3)$ remains zero until $t=3,$ at which point it jumps to $1.$\n\nWhen a second-order differential equation includes a term involving $u_a(t),$ we still begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and solving for $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ (for $s>0$). The left-hand side is handled exactly as before, using the Laplace transforms of derivatives and the given initial conditions.\n\nThe main new idea is how the unit step function affects the transform of the forcing term. We will see how to account for this delay and how it changes the algebraic equation for $Y(s).$ To do this, we first need to determine the transform of the step function itself. Let's find some Laplace transforms." :tutorial/id #uuid "932cf723-31f5-5d76-95e4-d384feb59c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14810 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3d26acf-ed42-5d5f-9088-64b3a8a9e309" :step/math-academy-id 612666 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232000 :tutorial/content "Consider a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing, such as $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n**Step 1: Transform the left-hand side.**\n\nWe begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides. Let $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Using the formula for the second derivative and the initial conditions, we have $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0) = s^2Y(s).$$\n\n**Step 2: Transform the right-hand side.**\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, the unit step function satisfies $$\\mathcal L\\{u_2(t)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s},$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\n**Step 3: Solve for $Y(s).$**\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}.$$ We now collect terms and solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAside from the exponential factor $e^{-2s},$ the steps are exactly the same as in problems without time delay." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7be86fb-5367-564f-906a-5ce064205685" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14834 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "f07d73a0-6354-56ab-81e4-4b5131af3c8c" :step/math-academy-id 612667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231403 :question/id #uuid "7ff7cc10-3649-5ffe-9a8a-6aedd7954ea5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0baa73e5-19ee-5a80-bd02-cc9678615d60" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049950}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377452}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "8145dfa8-c039-550b-be43-1fa940e25360" :step/math-academy-id 612668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231998 :tutorial/content "In the first-order case, we saw that a time delay in the forcing term appears in the $s$-domain as a factor of $e^{-as},$ and that we can handle this using the second shifting theorem.\n\nThe same idea applies when working with **second-order** differential equations.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + 4y = 7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}, \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nAgain, we begin by taking the Laplace transform of both sides and writing $$Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$$ Using the initial conditions, the left-hand side becomes $$\\mathcal L\\{y''\\}+4\\mathcal L\\{y\\}=s^2Y(s)+4Y(s).$$\n\nThe forcing term has the form $$u_2(t)\\,f(t-2),$$ where $$f(t-2)=7e^{-2(t-2)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad f(t)=7e^{-2t}.$$\n\nAs in the first-order case, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. That is, if $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$$ then $$\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}=e^{-as}F(s),$$ which is valid for $s>0.$\n\nHere, using $a=2,$ we have $$F(s)=\\mathcal L\\{7e^{-2t}\\}=\\frac{7}{s+2},$$ so the transformed forcing term is $$\\mathcal L\\{7u_2(t)e^{-2(t-2)}\\} = e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{7}{s+2}.$$\n\nSubstituting into the transformed differential equation gives $$s^2Y(s)+4Y(s)=\\frac{7e^{-2s}}{s+2}.$$ We now solve algebraically for $Y(s){:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{s+2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-2s}}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+4)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nJust as in the first-order case, the delay introduces the factor $e^{-as}.$ Once this factor is identified, the remaining steps follow the same algebraic process used for second-order equations without time delay." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7d1588f-46ce-5a23-a449-ceb6b8f25099" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14829 :tutorial/title "Time-Delayed Forcing and the Second Shifting Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "b88d3621-4e32-596f-9b38-b53f3da4d2ad" :step/math-academy-id 612671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231301 :question/id #uuid "9941d046-be37-5ada-8d98-ecedfcb9d7f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21904"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15ae93ce-c4dd-5b33-b9ff-381502364213" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049492}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518201}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Time-Delayed Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "0947db02-2bd3-50a0-803f-4e78e3ce7311" :step/math-academy-id 612672 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231999 :tutorial/content "Once the Laplace transform has been found, solving a second-order initial value problem with step function forcing follows the same overall strategy as before: we take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nThe key difference is handling terms that include a factor of $e^{-as}$.\n\nConsider the initial value problem $$y'' + y = u_2(t), \\qquad y(0)=0,\\qquad y'(0)=0,$$ where $u_2(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nWe previously found the Laplace transform $$Y(s)=e^{-2s}\\cdot\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$\n\nTo take the inverse Laplace transform, we first focus on the *base function* $$F(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}.$$ Using partial fraction decomposition, we write $$\\frac{1}{s(s^2+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}.$$\n\nNow we compute the inverse transform of $F(s)$ and call it $f(t){:}$ $$f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\!\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{s^2+1}\\right\\} = 1-\\cos t.$$\n\nNext, we apply the **second shifting theorem**. Since $Y(s)=e^{-2s}F(s)$, we have $$\\mathcal L^{-1}\\{e^{-2s}F(s)\\} = u(t-2)\\cdot f(t-2).$$ Substituting our result for $f(t)$, the solution to the initial value problem is $$y(t)=u(t-2)\\left(1-\\cos(t-2)\\right).$$\n\nThe important idea is that once the exponential factor $e^{-as}$ has been identified, we first find the inverse transform of the base function, then shift the result and multiply by the unit step function." :tutorial/id #uuid "10f6f136-0604-57e5-b635-1c8a5cfcf8da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14830 :tutorial/title "Solving for the Laplace Transform With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "3dff1d7e-fcee-5906-8723-2dbeac9f85ee" :step/math-academy-id 612676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231300 :question/id #uuid "029321f3-c767-5a77-95d7-5b4150e24f42" :question/math-academy-id "e-21903"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0459cf03-27db-5198-ab3a-1dbeecb675a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518206}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Initial Value Problem With Step Function Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "f8a1577f-f7d4-5c2b-869b-f0ae315317fa" :step/math-academy-id 612677}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231525}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311} :activity/id #uuid "8b4c0c6e-e1ad-53be-980f-1125809beccc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049590 :topic/id #uuid "02cc8363-d667-570b-9679-eb87a01109a9" :topic/math-academy-id 6728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070cb125-df6a-5a6e-a3c1-eebd12e71978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377475} {:answer/id #uuid "f4013812-37ba-5064-ab55-54d4b4b78e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-2s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377476} {:answer/id #uuid "b3afdc87-a5c1-502d-b40f-2421236c8cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} {:answer/id #uuid "436278c2-b580-52a1-8e49-b07bf5e41e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+4s+13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377478} {:answer/id #uuid "665b8f05-7e39-58e8-bd72-72806cf6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{9{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} :answer-field/id #uuid "443034ac-6f38-5490-9b31-4ff0cd1c4442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c8533a8-1f90-5b54-be02-41e34f166ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345255" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1)),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+4L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+13L\\{y\\} & =L\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the first derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)f(t-a)\\}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=1$ and $f(t-1)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))$, so $f(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L\\{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\}=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))\\}={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+4sY(s)+13Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4s+13)Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ed0242a-2db9-5144-8e11-5ba6887b8361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9575d503-b33a-5aaf-a793-2497c79dc90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{-5(t-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517769} :answer-field/id #uuid "5479ca0e-e235-5daf-9f78-3a7ac798d247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e0f1706f-269a-5658-8fd3-75993082b4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345300" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+6{y}^{′}(t)+5y(t)=20{u}_{4}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s)=\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+6sY(s)+5Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+6s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ (s+1)(s+5)Y(s) & =\\frac{20{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+1}+\\frac{C}{s+5}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=4,$ $B=-5,$ and $C=1,$ giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{s(s+1)(s+5)}=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{s}-\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{s+5},\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nf(t)=4-5{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-4s}F(s)\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{4}(t)\\,f(t-4) \\\\ y(t) & =u(t-4)\\cdot (4-5{e}^{-(t-4)}+{e}^{-5(t-4)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99b6edeb-bbfc-5c8b-b1c4-dd5efc3764aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a207cf-6ec4-5e6d-997d-39d88a243b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4db7a8-b2f3-5998-9cda-8fbb2aabb05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517567} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf6de9-76a1-580c-a0a5-04e50833b026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3s+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517752} {:answer/id #uuid "8f14cb64-f15c-5e63-97dc-cb8d52fe503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517757} {:answer/id #uuid "a5df1187-9902-5a00-900d-f4f5ec5cd0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{s({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517496} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d0813c-5c54-5890-9610-b5222646eaaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2f59173-bdb2-59d3-8bec-268e720e4bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345246" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{4}(t)=u(t-4)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+16y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n7{u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=4$ and $f(t)=7(t-4).$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{7u(t-4)\\cdot (t-4)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7((t+4)-4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot L\\{7t\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot 7\\cdot \\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+16L\\{y\\} & =L\\{7{u}_{4}(t)(t-4)\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-3+16Y(s) & =\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+16)Y(s) & =3+\\frac{7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+7{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b7f1db-a884-5b7a-8f66-cefcb30e2b44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462f1b1b-9444-58ff-b801-8fbbfcbcb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377461} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9218e-9c07-5797-9e3d-2114ea136be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377462} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30168a-f94d-52a2-9a98-bf5ae26c54ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377463} {:answer/id #uuid "5d96461b-b5bf-5858-b6b1-4e9dc9828ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} {:answer/id #uuid "b67556e4-5189-5cc7-aaee-81b6db402e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4669c7-fc85-5f41-a5f5-f8f6933fbb16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e512d666-26fd-5562-9dad-5a955b3c727f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345242" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+9y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5(t-3)^{2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u(t-3)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5((t+3)-3)^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{5{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+9L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{3}(t)(t-3)^{2}\\} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)+9Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+9)Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9df5258-7677-5050-9ab9-8650f43b3255" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35b6c1e-f747-5862-8863-fe053c5aa6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377482} {:answer/id #uuid "143176f2-5f66-5c47-bc95-252c5655a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\cos(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517527} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c74efd7-0f5a-5f39-afee-3b02924def1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3731bc55-d35c-5b26-9516-425a707e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-345263" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)={u}_{1}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-1)\\,\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1})\\} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{1}(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-1)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb271466-d784-5171-b57b-f15347d64110" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518277}}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs With Time-Delayed Forcing Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517488}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222306} :activity/id #uuid "4bdf9815-fa54-50ce-b3f5-ad6d001dd7d2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050054 :topic/id #uuid "29ce72ae-dbea-5d83-a81d-90545461cdec" :topic/math-academy-id 6375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222473 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to compute the **matrix exponential**.\n\nRecall that the scalar exponential function $e^t$ can be written as the power series \n\n$$\ne^t = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{t^k}{k!} = 1 + t + \\dfrac{t^2}{2} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nNow, let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix. By definition, the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ is given by the analogous series\n\n$$\ne^{At} = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!} = I + tA + \\dfrac{t^2}{2}A^2 + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!}A^k + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nThere are several approaches for computing matrix exponentials:\n\n- The most direct approach is to use the definition above, but this is typically impractical.\n\n- Another method, often covered in linear algebra courses, is to use the Jordan canonical form of $A$. This works, but it can also be tedious, especially for larger matrices.\n\nHere, we will introduce a different method.\n\nBy the Cayley–Hamilton theorem, every power $A^n, A^{n+1}, \\ldots$ can be rewritten as a linear combination of \n\n$$\nI, \\: A, \\: A^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: A^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, $e^{At}$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1}A^{n-1} t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_0, \\alpha_1, \\ldots, \\alpha_{n-1}$ are functions of $t$ and $I$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix. \n\nNow, we define the polynomial \n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0.\n$$\n\nThen we have the following key fact:\n\n> For any eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of $At$, we have $r(\\lambda) = e^{\\lambda}.$\n\nLet’s now see how this method works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "d78b3664-4117-56fc-bf30-5ca3acb28377" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f932ab79-9fa6-5a87-aa94-755b1685c4e3" :step/math-academy-id 588472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222482 :tutorial/content "Let's find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic polynomial $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we can find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7.\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t) &={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5t\\alpha_1 = e^{7t} - e^{2t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{5t}\\bigl(e^{7t}-e^{2t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $7$ and subtracting twice the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5\\alpha_0 = 7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\bigl(7e^{2t}-2e^{7t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((2{e}^{7t}-2{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((7{e}^{7t}-7{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 7({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) & 5{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e5aa695-b237-54d4-9836-20514d70dc9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14389 :tutorial/title "2x2 Matrices With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3c91134a-154b-5692-a2cc-fdc45c81af7e" :step/math-academy-id 588473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221150 :question/id #uuid "35a158c3-37b6-5123-ba90-1fd05218ce43" :question/math-academy-id "e-21271"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308645}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ebe4cfdc-9e1a-577d-ad38-9d117b4cf820" :step/math-academy-id 588474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221151 :question/id #uuid "18064c11-63d3-599a-b504-7b589f46c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532679}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ea0b2d60-3227-5e25-b6a9-171c0dc66319" :step/math-academy-id 588478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222481 :tutorial/content "Recall that the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ can be written as\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1} A^{n-1}t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I.\n$$\n\nIf we define the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0,\n$$\n\nthen for any eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ of $At,$ we have\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda_i) = e^{\\lambda_i}.\n$$\n\nHowever, for *repeated eigenvalues*, we have the following additional fact:\n\n> If $\\lambda_i$ is an eigenvalue of multiplicity $k$ with $k>1,$ then \n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{\\lambda }^{2}}r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}},\\,…,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{k-1}}{\\text{d}{\\lambda }^{k-1}}r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}}\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n\nLet's apply these facts to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d7b15b25-b73f-5d5f-aa02-e4c1a5e9804e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14388 :tutorial/title "2x2 Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "855a741b-2bbe-5e04-b892-8d1d9c7d0e65" :step/math-academy-id 588481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221410 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221152 :question/id #uuid "40a6318a-a1c9-55f3-afdb-02b43891d7b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "de5a7d5c-0c01-5247-8ba6-a69a863c9cbb" :step/math-academy-id 588482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222505 :tutorial/content "Again, let $A$ be a constant $n\\times n$ matrix. The matrix exponential satisfies\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\bigl(e^{At}\\bigr)=A\\,e^{At}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, recall that\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(At)^k}{k!}\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we differentiate the series above term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}\\cdot k{t}^{k-1}}{k!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k-1}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{j=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{j}{t}^{j}}{j!} \\\\  & =A\\,{e}^{At}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "739548ab-310c-5de3-a426-1fcaa3282478" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14526 :tutorial/title "Derivatives of Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "fc10fbf4-2be0-5b3f-ad2e-0505b175d5a6" :step/math-academy-id 588485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221411 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221194 :question/id #uuid "c0213c76-94e8-5ab3-a260-8a78a205589a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370321}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "df4956ab-0323-513e-ab21-66734987f4d0" :step/math-academy-id 588486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222506 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials have other properties. We list a few of them:\n\n- *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n$$\ne^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n$$\n\n- *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n(e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n$$\n\n- *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n$$\ne^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n$$\n\n- *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n$$\ne^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fcc3801-5d58-5c8e-b8c8-7be65b34f71a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14527 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "24b8ab46-a4b1-5358-abbd-db086f102dbf" :step/math-academy-id 588489}] :activity/title "Matrix Exponentials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221467}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532736} :activity/id #uuid "e67bdbbc-b2ac-5dc2-96db-49866bc692da" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050054 :topic/id #uuid "29ce72ae-dbea-5d83-a81d-90545461cdec" :topic/math-academy-id 6375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a54273-3450-5c26-84d7-383486f21cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307277} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db64a2d-0768-5ffd-ba7e-05c9f4485c48" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec22e530-554e-5020-8783-f257d54f500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307324} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cf4951-3613-5027-8a9a-0372890bd5d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79079e07-e6bc-5f6a-8ed1-2ff769b8b920" :question/math-academy-id "q-334729" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3t$ | $1-3t$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(3t) &={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t) &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03d5d0e2-58f6-580f-b08d-c488e5333a32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a8db4e8-f732-5d22-9704-09e0ccf298b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(3t)+\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3247c73e-d754-5617-80f3-a0beacefea90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84eb7c6-9806-5d01-aade-ce5ce09883c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532472} :answer-field/id #uuid "4788b925-6ee1-5536-8ffa-a6478b71d51d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "900adaef-859d-5d14-9981-202d2269e069" :question/math-academy-id "q-334626" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+3\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-3\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "447659a5-783c-55e2-b7bc-7044e79e9190" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7805cc61-032d-5340-b315-43d00f9ec949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306291} :answer-field/id #uuid "5694d036-6e3a-56ea-beeb-680490a87b8c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aacf013a-2689-512b-8794-bcbacddaf843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306764} :answer-field/id #uuid "674d0fa8-85cf-5f7e-bdaa-2d7ca81bb93d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f29b7109-5c33-5046-8236-7d693dea5b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-334634" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 3\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "783d8e6f-d461-5f0b-8f11-aa192fbe52a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b1d7ae-e4dd-5799-9115-e6e7b1fd12ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305951} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dde19ef-9af0-5255-a3d4-655b000140c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307818} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc7ea02-06bd-5e6c-aed2-4751d80186b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305289} :answer-field/id #uuid "893c4189-3f31-573e-94ae-26b874312a53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bf1a0f0-bb87-5dac-ad3b-1ce93c87e2a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334733" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1-6t$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t) &={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t) &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =8{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1+6t) \\\\ 12{\\alpha }_{1}t & =12{e}^{2t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+6t & -3t \\\\ 12t & 1-6t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc3bbe46-b9a2-5b99-9ca0-73318c047651" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753cf168-9af8-50a8-bf6d-aec9b1e3c2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}(7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306266} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e34fb24-26d6-54f8-8e2c-e459754da21c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa9799e7-8c6d-5377-a47d-4e6a05e2b186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}(7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306762} :answer-field/id #uuid "4609b934-301d-57c7-89ed-e8cdd2469bcc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f24e94e-6875-5dc6-95dc-6ad3d893da7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{sin}(7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305283} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f2f7b3-4e91-5fb5-bc50-5086640214d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680c33fd-8ce1-50c7-9c3f-e14b4d6e395c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{sin}(7t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306145} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d0c784-8e29-5661-9d95-8147778f15de" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14f4f5f9-2f59-5657-8bee-d1931d1011cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335107" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & -7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eabd75d2-306c-5778-a5d4-6908c275c489"}] :activity/title "Matrix Exponentials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531935}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230029} :activity/id #uuid "7bded8c9-0cf0-5f07-a1df-46efbe5e773e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050118 :topic/id #uuid "2e807778-73d8-5eb5-8321-280484cf5ee2" :topic/math-academy-id 6686} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230153 :tutorial/content "We know how to apply the implicit Euler method to initial value problems\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthat are linear in $y,$ that is, where $f(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x).$\nHowever, in general, $f$ can be a nasty, nonlinear function in $y.$ For example, consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2 - xy^4,\\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCan we apply the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 0.5$ to this IVP to approximate the value of $y(0.5)?$\n\nYes, we can! However, doing so requires defining a function $g$ to which the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a solution of the equation $g(y_\\text{new})=0.$ Then, we apply a root-finding method to solve for $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nTo see how, let's start by applying the implicit Euler method as we normally would. In general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into the update formula $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4} \\\\ 0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with terms such as $0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4$ and $y_\\text{new},$ there’s no simple algebraic rearrangement, and solving exactly is impractical.\n\nInstead, let's define a new function $g$ of $y_\\text{new}$ by the expression on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^4 + y_\\text{new} - y - 0.5(x_\\text{new})^2\n$$\n\nNote that, at any step, $y$ and $x_\\text{new}$ are known constants, so $g$ is truly a one-variable function in $y_\\text{new}.$\n\nThen, the next $y$-value $y_\\text{new}$ is a root of this function: $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ Therefore, to find $y_\\text{new},$ we apply a root-finding method, such as Newton's method.\n\nFor instance, in the first step, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new}=0+0.5=0.5.$ So, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5\\cdot 0.5\\cdot ({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-0.5\\cdot (0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{4}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we would proceed to use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "84c89e00-0b30-5678-80a1-4471ec2526da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14291 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "53c7c39e-1860-5264-97bb-c0c9c308b10f" :step/math-academy-id 594477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228831 :question/id #uuid "185f4059-c0d2-59a0-932b-d1152c407db6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050483}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391137}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"} :step/id #uuid "8e83164c-b12f-53b6-8bf8-1839b27d9361" :step/math-academy-id 594478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228832 :question/id #uuid "2e45e65d-ffd1-5418-9445-eee71cb36ded" :question/math-academy-id "e-21319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308434}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"} :step/id #uuid "312607c0-b5af-5df8-9d97-f0233697b8a9" :step/math-academy-id 594481}] :activity/title "Using the Implicit Euler Method With Newton's Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229254}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222338} :activity/id #uuid "799f17ca-e712-547a-8b6c-91fc224e0a3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050611 :topic/id #uuid "58ba8166-5e45-548c-9143-2c0d455f1d66" :topic/math-academy-id 6400} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222489 :tutorial/content "Can we apply Euler's method to approximate solutions to second-order initial value problems, such as the one below?\n\n$$\nxy'' - y' - y = 2x, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 1\n$$\n\nWell, for a second-order initial value problem,\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x, y, y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{'} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δz &={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nIn the example above, we set up Euler's method by first isolating $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{''}-{y}^{'}-y & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{''} & =y+{y}^{'}+2x \\\\ {y}^{''} & =\\frac{y+{y}^{'}}{x}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z=y',$ $z'=y''$ in the above. This gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will approximate values for this system using step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d9782a2-388c-59cd-bb4e-165619f75de3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14485 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbcb5ea9-acb5-53ee-a98c-a6a1f2ee7329" :step/math-academy-id 594421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221173 :question/id #uuid "81a8347f-7852-5413-8d96-946ec658929e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050629}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365633}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "ba8deacf-a74a-5730-a2a4-637d1cc352aa" :step/math-academy-id 594422 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221176 :question/id #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365645}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"} :step/id #uuid "c9df785c-d2e4-5fb4-ab71-6b247003510d" :step/math-academy-id 594425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221177 :question/id #uuid "25d6bf3b-282e-5c8b-b665-923dd602849d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21669"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391364}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"} :step/id #uuid "5277b709-6b41-5099-b1a2-e16d03ed909a" :step/math-academy-id 594428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222529 :tutorial/content "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" :tutorial/id #uuid "23470bd2-647f-5c35-b30d-10e88b4b7c3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14856 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "2035b256-5c13-5188-9464-cbf223c96e9d" :step/math-academy-id 606510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222339}}] :activity/title "Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221479}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222294} :activity/id #uuid "0db2ac37-787f-5ca2-b7bf-324550d93a1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222498 :tutorial/content "Each step of a numerical method produces a local truncation error $\\tau,$ which, over multiple steps, accumulates into a global truncation error $e.$ Both LTE and GTE depend on step size $\\Delta x.$\n\nThe **order** of a numerical method describes how fast these errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\nA numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the *largest positive* integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(\\frac{Δx}{k}) & ≈K{(\\frac{Δx}{k})}^{p}=\\frac{K(Δx)^{p}}{{k}^{p}}≈\\frac{e(Δx)}{{k}^{p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate with an example. Suppose a numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.21,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.21) = 0.785.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.07,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.07) = 0.010.$\n\nUsing this data, we can estimate the order of the numerical method.\n\nThe step size decreases from $0.21$ to $0.07.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.21}{0.07} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 3^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.785$ to $0.010.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.785}{0.010} = 78.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈78.5 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(78.5) \\\\ p & ≈3.97….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$\n\nBut why is order important? Well, a higher order means that the error decreases faster as $\\Delta x$ decreases. As a result, higher-order methods can achieve a target accuracy *with fewer steps*. In this way, order predicts the accuracy you gain when you make steps smaller." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e84a8b-32df-5566-99ff-2b37f338d69f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ec16a00b-18c7-517a-94d1-35859655f930" :step/math-academy-id 585132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221184 :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443240}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"} :step/id #uuid "8b58215f-a6df-5452-b05c-85bfbc0fd9f2" :step/math-academy-id 585133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222499 :tutorial/content "An alternative, but equivalent, definition for the **order** can be made in terms of the local truncation error.\n\nA numerical method has **order** $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has **order** $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nBut why is the power $p+1$ for LTE but only $p$ for GTE?\n\nEach local truncation error measures the error made in *one step* of the method. If a method has order $p,$ then the error made in a single step shrinks like\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, on a fixed interval, using step size $\\Delta x$ requires about\n\n$$\nN \\approx \\dfrac1{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nsteps. The global truncation error results from *accumulating* these local errors over all steps.\n\nHeuristically, adding up $N$ local errors of size $(\\Delta x)^{p+1}$ gives\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\sim N \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} \\sim \\dfrac1{\\Delta x} \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} = (\\Delta x)^p.\n$$\n\nThus, the local truncation error is one power of $\\Delta x$ higher than the global truncation error.\n\nNext, we will apply this definition to determine the order of a given numerical method." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5d4a6f3-fc92-557f-be1f-b14832313c21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14501 :tutorial/title "Order From Local Truncation Error"} :step/id #uuid "7d7d7ca0-178c-5ff6-b4a4-b9cd8fd8f8c0" :step/math-academy-id 585136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221185 :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512951}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"} :step/id #uuid "c0cc635d-3c16-5296-b225-2c8ad2a20267" :step/math-academy-id 585137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221186 :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535065}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"} :step/id #uuid "4725b391-1504-568d-8f34-bad7e7d66adf" :step/math-academy-id 585140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221187 :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512904}] :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"} :step/id #uuid "76c4b12b-8383-5ed3-9f8c-d559ed16dcd4" :step/math-academy-id 585144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222512 :tutorial/content "We'll now show that the definition of order with respect to LTE is the same as with respect to GTE.\n\nIndeed, suppose a numerical method with increment formula $\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x$ satisfies the following:\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nOn a fixed interval of length $L,$ the number of steps is $N \\approx \\dfrac{L}{\\Delta x}.$ So,\n$N = \\mathcal O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\Delta x}\\right)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$\n\nNow, recall the relationship between GTE and LTE, given by\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N = e_{N-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nSince $y(x_{N-1}) - y_{N-1} = e_{N-1},$ it is natural to say that changing the input $y$ by $e_{N-1}$ changes $\\Phi$ by about a constant multiple of $e_{N-1}.$ So, heuristically,\n\n$$\n\\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\approx Ce_{N-1}\n$$\n\nfor some $C$ that *doesn't* depend on $\\Delta x$ (on a fixed interval). Hence, the GTE satisfies approximately\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)e_{N-1} + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nAfter $N$ steps, we get\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)^N e_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^N(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}\\tau_n.\n$$\n\nNext, note that $(1+C\\Delta x)^N \\approx e^{CN\\Delta x} \\approx e^{CL},$ which is $\\mathcal O(1)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ So, the weights $(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}$ don't change the *power* of $\\Delta x.$\n\nSo, we can treat the GTE as roughly the sum of the per-step local errors. Therefore,\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx \\sum_{n=1}^N \\tau_n\n$$\n\nWith $\\tau_n = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)$ for each $n=1,\\ldots,N,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(Δx) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =N\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,(\\frac{1}{Δx})\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,((Δx)^{p}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p$ is maximal in the LTE definition, this shows the GTE has order $p$ as well." :tutorial/id #uuid "380a28f7-f36f-50b0-982b-365f87b068bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14656 :tutorial/title "Connecting the Definitions of the Order of Numerical Methods"} :step/id #uuid "a05640d9-1389-5d1b-b299-1230e633c270" :step/math-academy-id 593404}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221478}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620} :activity/id #uuid "5f5a0a27-74df-55fb-b257-c512fcb73cb8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869} :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c42e845-2d37-5c62-8435-55cbffc5e702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} {:answer/id #uuid "336b0018-6868-5229-a732-914282396c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a27cbe-7a31-5696-93a4-64fa5ae5e5c9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d80c5e-1df7-5ab6-a247-0eedf2a21825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462287} {:answer/id #uuid "bc61ed5b-b2b1-5bf5-89e2-98973109ac8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb95d659-7aa2-5922-b590-ce7333f86a7a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb8c5c2f-7a3c-5fc5-8d79-d95df07bb8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} {:answer/id #uuid "f7308828-7ddd-5c00-8a47-b3eec31e20ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27c7694-a58b-5f51-a22b-60b4c92a4e89" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f2ba467-6ea9-5201-b847-1a519dc1ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333576" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2823fe-ac2e-533e-b8ef-d3d1dec21d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf20e996-1ea4-5bae-aecf-b59e8d34708e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a7b9303-06ef-5983-bd7d-577c4a789987" :question/math-academy-id "q-334946" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b510a71-8c9b-527d-9bcb-f5fbb3d49293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396439} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} {:answer/id #uuid "7357b661-3a8e-