value "102.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396452} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8aca3b-0007-5700-a38f-b489a38acf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396453} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c50b5-edcd-59fc-87f9-4fe83145da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "107.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396454} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fcccd7-bafd-5c90-975c-73cad92fbe95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455} :answer-field/id #uuid "b053a512-93f4-5783-be56-305d82bb8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b7c966-24c0-5f9e-ba19-26d61189aa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396457} {:answer/id #uuid "b09802b2-85c8-57e5-8e51-14a3469d9ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396458} {:answer/id #uuid "7a131701-833d-535b-800c-3887b7fc4a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396459} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d8158d-f371-5bb8-9048-14d8a5c82403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e74d-7332-5d6c-91a3-aaeed8c4c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5e6e5a-102a-53f8-be70-6fec86c3cf6a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481769}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513006} :activity/id #uuid "9cbd980a-784d-5a1b-93c3-51df50e05a4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512184} :answer-field/id #uuid "290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334826" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.3125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.3125}{39.0625}=0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03959187-9418-5be5-ac79-7e4967d9b00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512109} {:answer/id #uuid "585966c4-0f5a-5998-b590-6b01a0ed43b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512301} {:answer/id #uuid "82ff2bc6-4ad2-5485-9688-6c45d0274b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512405} {:answer/id #uuid "c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512563} {:answer/id #uuid "c735b7b9-b2be-5342-be94-4a2ee43a5709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512563} :answer-field/id #uuid "01277570-5a55-57cd-b355-f92266d0d444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334747" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17647057-986e-5b9c-a8af-af759b573b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4007ccd-444f-5475-a4c0-493bff543247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e11ea327-0283-5223-89c9-1dcc1d605dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-334763" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} {:answer/id #uuid "713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512369} {:answer/id #uuid "7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512375} {:answer/id #uuid "a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512500} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :question/math-academy-id "q-334642" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424a9e92-70d2-56e9-9aed-a2fa1212a8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512238} {:answer/id #uuid "5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512308} {:answer/id #uuid "6d96eeb8-a870-5867-9c64-0e82e01c23ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512358} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c4497b-4049-5d0b-93c0-4a718cf248a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512531} {:answer/id #uuid "f6852f41-c0ac-53c8-b2e9-1ad22c6166af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512308} :answer-field/id #uuid "27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-334749" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{4}=3(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{5}=7(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512992}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512090}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535126} :activity/id #uuid "9cb47b16-2af6-58f1-8c90-123557ce104c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869} :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "003358a9-f2af-59ba-b62d-17d3096b53b8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cb8cbf-2525-5aa2-adfe-cd481e3a035f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305203} {:answer/id #uuid "21bfa1f7-f19d-56ce-b7f8-e855ea310574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305475} {:answer/id #uuid "2eabd2c9-c08e-5c90-8a9a-e97ba53e2e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305595} {:answer/id #uuid "7458f81a-c783-575e-87db-c6a1d925d0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306256} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4785ce-99ec-52cb-ac88-d882b4bd6fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305475} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a56bc7-802d-5130-a340-47dd3d54165b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8e9fee6-366d-51d7-82a2-1629aefe0b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-334647" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "27637d35-ea70-516e-ae4d-2f2b9a81ce00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41acf3ec-3f1c-53ba-b201-c8a37ecb1876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534390} {:answer/id #uuid "44ca1322-93dc-59cc-b6ff-a1b25b2d993e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534395} {:answer/id #uuid "6f38908e-4bd6-5384-9925-30477dcb4253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534498} {:answer/id #uuid "a9dc44c0-ba2d-5163-911a-1574cea8f06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534644} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a3190f-7df3-50ea-a76a-7f1032b8c225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534390} :answer-field/id #uuid "986ff1d6-a817-5ff7-8aed-97a0eafbf95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c354aefb-fffc-5ecc-8cac-e2625bd63b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-334744" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "77786643-b29c-5c9c-ac94-7ecbfe592858" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d077e1b-69a5-5689-b7f2-27863f84eb6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9664d18-5c13-5c23-a303-4d40a0de2cc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d047c5c7-9757-5ee5-9e01-a79118905089" :question/math-academy-id "q-334796" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77da484d-f99e-564b-b4c3-19d24cde8a96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c4ecef28-9728-5f9f-986f-392086d6b9d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535102}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534259}]}
